# Copy to .env (git-ignored) and fill in real values. Never commit the real .env.
#
# Only relevant for running the root docker-compose.yml (the deployment stack) by hand --
# Compose auto-loads a `.env` next to the compose file for `${...}` interpolation.
# server/docker-compose.yml also loads it, but marks it optional -- the dev stack runs
# fine with none of this set.
#
# The real (Portainer-deployed) stack never reads this file: it has no `env_file:`
# directive, deliberately (see docker-compose.yml's header comment), and Portainer
# doesn't write one either. Set these same names in the Portainer stack's own
# "Environment variables" instead.

# --- Required by the deployment stack ----------------------------------------
# Postgres superuser password. The deployment stack builds DATABASE_URL from it rather
# than taking a whole connection string, so this is the only place it appears.
# Changing it after the first `up` does NOT re-key an existing database: Postgres only
# reads POSTGRES_PASSWORD when it initialises an empty data directory.
DB_PASSWORD=

# --- Optional overrides -------------------------------------------------------
# Identity provider credentials for the guest -> account upgrade (server/src/principal.ts,
# server/src/identity/). An unset pair simply omits the provider from
# `identity/registry.ts`'s map, and server/src/routes/identity.ts returns 503
# oauth_not_configured for its /api/auth/:provider/start and /callback routes only, same as
# the dev stack. The app's callback URLs must match API_URL below.
#
# Generic OIDC provider (e.g. a Supabase Cloud project). OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME is the slug
# used in the /api/auth/:provider/* routes and as `players.kind`'s linked-identity source;
# defaults to "oidc" if unset. The frontend no longer hardcodes this name -- it reads the
# configured slug back from /api/me (server/src/routes/session.ts) -- so any value here is
# safe; there is nothing else that needs to match it (issue #16).
OIDC_ISSUER_URL=
OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
# OIDC_PROVIDER_NAME=supabase
# Supabase Cloud's token endpoint doesn't RFC-decode client Basic-auth credentials, so a
# UUID client id sent the spec-compliant way is rejected -- raw (non-spec) Basic auth is
# therefore the default (server/src/identity/vendor-quirks.ts), load-bearing for this
# deployment. Only set this if the configured issuer is a normal, spec-compliant one that
# needs the RFC 6749 §2.3.1 encoding instead -- Supabase is not that.
# OIDC_SPEC_COMPLIANT_BASIC_AUTH=true

# Both default to the production hostnames in docker-compose.yml. SITE_URL is the browser
# origin allowed through CORS and the post-OAuth redirect target; API_URL is this server's
# own public origin, used to build the OAuth callback.
# SITE_URL=https://adventures.subzerodev.com
# API_URL=https://adventures-api.subzerodev.com

# Extra browser origins allowed to call this API on top of SITE_URL, comma-separated --
# the rapid-preview loop in docs/preview.md is what this is for. Unset (the default) is
# the original posture: SITE_URL and nothing else.
#
# Two things worth knowing before adding one. Every origin here can *write* as the signed-in
# player, not just read, so it must be a host you control. And a preview under
# *.subzerodev.com is what keeps sign-in working: the session cookie is host-only on this
# API's domain with SameSite=Lax, so a sibling subdomain still sends it while
# http://localhost:5173 (same-site to nothing) does not -- localhost previews are anonymous
# play only. Sign in on the real site once; the preview inherits that session.
# PREVIEW_ORIGINS=https://dev.adventures.subzerodev.com

# Pin a specific image instead of the moving `:latest` tag -- deploy-api.yml pushes a
# `:<sha>` tag for every build, which is what makes a rollback a one-line change here.
# ADVENTURES_API_IMAGE=ghcr.io/the-running-dev/adventures-api:latest

# The operator channel (server/src/discussions/, /discussions) over this repository's own
# GitHub Discussions. All three must be set or the feature is simply off -- an unset trio
# omits the forum entirely (discussions/registry.ts), and /api/discussions answers 503
# not_configured rather than blocking deployment on it. No provider name appears outside
# discussions/ itself, same rule the identity seam holds for OIDC_* above.
#
# DISCUSSIONS_REPO is "owner/repo", e.g. the-running-dev/SubZeroDev.Adventures -- a
# malformed value throws at startup rather than silently reporting "not configured".
# DISCUSSIONS_CATEGORY is the Discussions category's slug (its "General" tab, say); it must
# already exist on the repository and allow member-created discussions, or every post will
# 503. DISCUSSIONS_TOKEN must be a fine-grained personal access token scoped to this one
# repository with Discussions read+write and nothing else -- this token lives in the same
# process that parses player-supplied JSON and fetches player-supplied URLs, so a classic
# token with broad `repo` scope would be a code-push credential sitting next to untrusted
# input.
# DISCUSSIONS_REPO=the-running-dev/SubZeroDev.Adventures
# DISCUSSIONS_CATEGORY=general
# DISCUSSIONS_TOKEN=

# Admin access (server/src/routes/admin.ts) is a role on the player, not an env var --
# there is nothing to set here. Grant the first admin once, after the stack is up, by
# running the CLI against the deployed database (find your own provider:subject the same
# way the old ADMIN_SUBJECTS note here used to suggest -- sign in once, then read it back
# from the identities table):
#   docker compose run --rm api node dist/server/src/grant-role-cli.js <provider> <subject>
# Every admin after that is granted from the admin page itself.
